5:[["$","$L10","3.11",{"topic":{"id":"3.11","title":"Email Forensics","minutes":15,"summary":"Read headers, authentication results, and message context together to distinguish technical observations from claims about the sender.","sections":[{"title":"Preserve the complete message","body":["A raw message or supported mailbox export can preserve headers, MIME structure, attachments, and encoding that a screenshot omits. Record the mailbox, folder, retrieval method, account control, and export settings. Preserve attachment hashes separately and keep the original message unchanged. Do not open suspicious attachments on an ordinary workstation.","Headers describe a processing path, often through multiple servers. The visible From address can differ from the envelope sender in Return-Path. Received lines are generally read from top to bottom as later-to-earlier hops, but untrusted portions can be forged. Message-ID and Date fields are not guaranteed proof of origin or actual sending time."]},{"title":"SPF, DKIM, and DMARC","body":["SPF evaluates whether a sending path is authorized for the envelope domain, commonly reflected in Return-Path. DKIM uses a cryptographic signature over selected message content and headers; a valid signature shows that the signing domain signed the surviving signed material, not that the message is benign. DMARC evaluates identifier alignment with the visible From domain under its policy and results.","A pass does not prove that the visible sender is a trusted person. A legitimate but compromised domain can pass authentication. A fail can arise from forwarding or configuration problems, although it remains a useful warning. Inspect the actual Authentication-Results produced by a trusted receiver and understand the alignment, not just the words “pass” or “fail”."]},{"title":"Fraud reconstruction and response","body":["Compare the message’s request with known business process, prior payment instructions, and independently verified contact details. Look for changed bank details, urgency, secrecy, and unusual reply routing. These are risk indicators, not a complete legal proof of cheating. Correlate mailbox audit logs, sign-in events, payment records, and recipient actions.","If compromise is suspected, preserve evidence before coordinated containment, secure accounts, revoke sessions where appropriate, and notify the relevant response team. Contact the bank through an official channel for urgent transfers. A classroom header exercise does not send email, contact a server, or test a real account."]}],"practice":{"title":"The urgent account change","scenario":"A message displays “Supplier Finance” but uses a different From domain and a new beneficiary account.","steps":["Read the full headers and identify From, Return-Path, and authentication results.","Explain what each pass or fail establishes and what it does not.","Verify the request using a previously known contact, not the message’s phone number.","Preserve the raw message and payment trail before changing the original evidence."],"debrief":"Authentication is one layer. Payment verification and account-control evidence often decide whether the request was genuinely authorized."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bns","bsa"],"sources":["itact","cert","bsa"],"labId":"email","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain preserve the complete message and apply it to an investigation.","Explain sPF, DKIM, and DMARC and apply it to an investigation.","Explain fraud reconstruction and response and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.11","prompt":"What does a valid DKIM signature establish?","options":["That the email is safe and truthful.","That the signing domain signed material that still verifies.","That the CEO personally wrote the message.","That no account could have been compromised."],"answer":1,"explanation":"DKIM provides cryptographic integrity and domain-signature information, not a guarantee of benign intent or human authorship.","unit":3}}}]]
